// Verbatim text of the OpenEVO Type D Mk4 Assembly Manual (github.com/Binomica-Labs/OpenEVO), one entry per numbered step.
export const MANUAL = {
 "pre": {
  "intro": "Before you want to build your own EasyEVO machine by yourself and start the assembly manual, there are some materials and wires that you have to prepare first! Remember to check all of the components. This move can spare you lots of time, and after it’s all set, it’s time for you to start the operation!",
  "printed": "Below is the list of all materials that you might use for: 3D Printed Modules for the robot's backbone, we will use a 3D printer.",
  "material": "The link refers to the materials that we’ll need to build the robot. The spreadsheet file is available on our GitHub:",
  "wirelist": "The link refers to wires that we’ll need to build the robot. The spreadsheet file is available on our GitHub:",
  "crimp1": "Crimp male pin headers onto the 6mm stripped wire ends by folding the connector flaps tightly using needle-nose pliers. Begin with the middle flaps, ensuring a secure grip on the exposed wire. This technique requires patience and precision; if a crimp is unsuccessful, simply restart to ensure high-quality connections. While the demonstration uses \"helping hands\" clips, you can easily hold the connectors by hand.",
  "crimp2": "Once crimped, slide the connector into its housing until it clicks into place. If you encounter resistance, use the pliers to gently squeeze the sides of the crimped area—perpendicular to your original fold—to improve the fit. Finish by giving the wire a light tug to confirm the housing is securely locked."
 },
 "hardware": {
  "1": "Let’s begin by assembling the robot’s rails and plugging them into Module A (LCD rail part). This will cradle the LCD Faceplate and interface buttons. Be sure the rods go all the way into the machine. The fit should be tight but not difficult to remove. If the rods are gripping too tightly (due to the ends being poorly cut at the factory), insert and remove the rods a few times to create a nice friction fit. Place the assembly on a leveled flat surface to check if both rods went in all the way.",
  "2": "Obtain the Module B (LCD Faceplate), two black buttons, and one blue button. Remove the washer and nut from the buttons, but save the nut as we will be using this shortly.",
  "3": "Place the buttons through the faceplate holes and secure them with nuts. The button closest to the top rim of Module B (LCD faceplate) may need to be positioned first, with the flat side against the wall, and then screwed in by hand. The other buttons can be fastened by holding the nut with needlenose pliers and turning it while holding the button firmly. Arrange it so the button order from top to bottom is: BLACK, BLUE, BLACK. Rotate the buttons such that their blades are vertically aligned.",
  "4": "Strip an 80mm piece of any color silicone wire such that it produces exposed wire long enough to cover the length from the bottom button blades to the top button blade, with a little extra to spare so you can easily grip the silicone covering and slide it off the wire bundle. Twist and tin this piece of wire, then solder it across one side of the button blades to bridge them. This will form the common ground for the buttons.",
  "5": "Prepare wires:\n34cm, black, solid-male x1\n36cm, green, solid-male x1\n37cm, white, solid-male x1\n38cm, blue, solid-male x1\n\nMount the LCD in Module B (LCD faceplate). Route the non-crimped ends through the small slot in Module A (LCD rail part). Arrange the wires such that they lie flat in the slot. And put the screen inside the box; it should be a good fit, and this might take more strength to push it in.",
  "6": "Arrange the wires such that the black wire is on the side of the slot furthest from the edge of the faceplate. Ideally, arrange all the wires such that the order of the wires from left to right is blue, white, green, black, and then solder them on the opposite side of the wire in step 4. This is optional but will result in a tidier fit. Solder the wires to the button blades as follows:\n\nBlue -> Button UP (top)\n\nWhite -> Button SEL (middle)\n\nGreen -> Button DOWN (bottom)\n\nBlack -> Common Ground (solder to the bottom button’s ground blade)",
  "7": "Connect the LCD Faceplate to the LC Rail, gently pulling on the wires to tighten slack as the parts mate. Ensure none of the wires get crushed during this process. Check to see if the Module B (LCD Faceplate) is fully pushed into the Module A (LCD rail). Compare your fit to the image below.",
  "8": "Apply a 42mm strip of Double-sided Tape (approx length of the module) to the flat back of the microSD module. Take off the protective tape carefully and apply the microSD module to the LCD Screen as shown below:",
  "9": "Prepare wires:\n30cm, black, female-male x1\n30cm, red, female-male x1\n38cm, pink, female-male x1\n38cm, brown, female-male x1\n38cm, orange, female-male x1\n38cm, purple, female-male x1\n\nConnect the wires to the microSD module as follows (sometimes the marker would probably mark on the back of the microSD module, so better to check before you stick the microSD module on it!):",
  "10": "Prepare wires:\n30cm, black, female-male x1\n30cm, red, female-male x1\n38cm, yellow, female-female x1\n38cm, green, female-female x1\n\nNext, we will make the wires for the LCD Screen’s I2C board. This connects the LCD screen to the robot via the I2C protocol, which uses just four wires.",
  "11": "Flip the entire robot upside down and apply rubber feet as shown in the image below. These will absorb vibrations, help keep the robot leveled, and add some clearance for wiring underneath the robot.",
  "12": "Obtain the Module C (Stirring Rail part), apply rubber feet to the underside as shown below (the center rubber foot is there to push back when firmly mounting the tube assembly later). Slide Module C (Stirring Rail) onto the robot's rails.",
  "13": "Grab the 40mm PC fan part and glue two Neodymium Magnets 12x6x3mm to the fan’s face using High-Temp RTV Silicone Sealant (or Gorilla Glue). Be sure to center the two magnets as shown. This is a VERY important part, so take the time to align the fan properly. Incubate the fan at 37℃ for 1 hour to cure the silicone or let it sit at room temp overnight.",
  "14": "Next up, we’re gonna build the Stirrer Base assembly (Older magnets shown). Starting with the fan, detach the red and black leads from each other and trim them down to 15mm. Strip 6mm, twist, and tin the exposed wire.",
  "15": "Cut 28cm of black and orange silicone wire. Strip 6mm from both ends of each wire, solder one end of each wire to the fan (orange to red, black to black), and apply 15mm of heat-shrink to the joint. Shrink the heat-shrink tubing with a hot-air gun or a lighter. Marine-grade heat shrink will ooze a small amount of sealant, which makes a waterproof seal around the joint. Be careful, as the sealant retains a lot of heat, and the joint will be very hot.",
  "16": "Trim 15mm of leads from the heater element, strip 6mm, twist, and tin the exposed wire.",
  "17": "Cut four 280mm pieces of orange wire. Strip 6mm from both ends of the four wires. Separate the wires into two pairs. Solder the tinned ends of one pair together, then solder each joined pair to one of the heater module's leads. Cut a 15mm piece of 2.4mm heat shrink tubing and seal the joint. Final result should be as shown below:",
  "18": "Unwind the leads of the NTC thermistor and pull them apart to about 40mm. Trim the leads to a total length of 40mm, with about 6mm of separation between leads. Using an emery board or nail file, scrape away the enamel coating of the thermistor leads, exposing about 6mm. The color will change if the enamel is removed, showing a pink-ish copper hue. Tin this region with a bit of solder. You’ll notice the enamel coating shrink away, leaving a small bit of residue. If the leads are tinned, the color will be a bright silver as seen below:",
  "19": "Using a helping hands, mount a 3-pin header onto one of the alligator clip arms, with the short side of the pin header facing outward. Take a 10K Precision Resistor, bend one lead inward, clip the leads, and solder them to one of the outer pins and the center pin as seen in the image below:",
  "20": "Solder the prepared NTC thermistor to the center pin, and the pin opposite of the one with a soldered resistor pin, as shown in the image below:",
  "21": "Using a marker, mark the pin that is not soldered to a resistor lead. Cut a 16mm piece of small 2.4mm diameter heatshrink and a 16mm piece of the 6mm diameter heatshrink tubing. Thread the small heatshrink piece over the thermistor wires to the base of the thermistor. Apply heat to shrink the tubing snug to the bottom of the thermistor. Place the larger heatshrink piece over all three pins of the thermometer assembly (resistor and thermistor). Make sure the large piece overlaps the black plastic body of the 3-pin header so that the heat-shrink covers the exposed soldered joints of the thermometer assembly, as shown in the image below.",
  "22": "While you’re at it, let’s make another thermistor assembly, but this time we’ll keep the thermistor leads long to make a thermometer needle. To start, let’s gather our supplies. We’ll need another NTC thermistor (no need to trim leads), a 3-pin male header, a 14-gauge 4” luer-lock dispensing needle, 20mm 9.6mm heatshrink, 44mm of 2.4mm heatshrink, and some black silicone sealant. Solder the resistor and thermistor leads to the short end of the 3-pin header as shown below (same as before). Then slide the 2.4mm heatshink piece over the thermistor to the base where the leads are soldered. Shrink it with a lighter or hot-air gun. Ensure it’s snug with the top of the leads. Slide the 9.6mm heatsink piece over the black plastic of the pin header and the resistor + thermistor joints such that it covers everything but does not overlap onto the metal of the long. Shrink the tubing so it looks like below. As the tubing heats, it melts the internal sealant, so once it cools a little, you can pinch the tubing to fuse the sealant around the parts for a nice waterproof seal. Be careful as the sealant is very hot!",
  "23": "Fill the inside of the needle’s luer lock fitting with black silicone sealant. Slide the thermistor into the dispensing needle until the heatshrink meets the bottom of the collar. Using a gloved hand, spread the silicone sealant around the luer-lock opening where the heatshrink touches the sealant such that it forms a smooth, uniform seal around the thermistor assembly. The final result should match the image below.",
  "24": "Now use a bit of black silicone sealant to fill the bottom opening of the needle so the thermistor tip is not exposed. This thermistor probe model has a sky-blue tip, so it’s easy to see if it’s sticking out. Be sure the sealant completely covers the hole, then flatten it into the tip as shown below. Incubate the thermistor needle at room temp for a few hours to allow the silicone to cure. You can speed this up by incubating at 37℃ for an hour.",
  "25": "You have now completed the thermistor needle and can move on to the next step. Congrats! Set this aside; we will need it later.\n\nLet’s now shift gears back to the Stirrer Base to attach the thermistor and heater module. Apply some silicone sealant to the groove inside the Module D (Stirrer Base part) as well as a blob across the slot of said part.",
  "26": "Slide the heater module into the sealant-filled slot. Make sure the module is pushed all the way to the back, with its corners tucked under the stirrer base slot's overhang. Wipe away any excess silicone sealant with a paper towel and a gloved hand.",
  "27": "Carefully slide the thermistor unit into the smaller slot beneath the heater module. Press it in until you feel the back wall of the slot pushing back. It might be stuck because the heat shrink is a little too big. Just reheat it and try to squeeze it to let it fit inside the box (it should be very hot after you reheat it, so be careful!). It should look like this:",
  "28": "Slide the fan from Step 20 into the slot at the bottom of Module D (Stirrer Base part), with the magnets facing upward. Invert the Stirrer Base and align the fan in the middle of the Stirrer Base. The Stirrer Base is now complete. Incubate the Stirrer Base at 37℃ for 1 hour or at room temperature overnight to fully cure the silicone sealant.",
  "29": "Next, let’s assemble the pump motors! Prepare wires:\n20cm, orange, solid-solid x8\n\nStrip back 6mm and tin both ends. Solder one wire to each motor terminal on all four pump motors as below. Cut 15mm of 2.4mm heat shrink and heat it at the junction of the wires and the motor terminals.",
  "30": "Using a black marker, mark the tinned end of the wire leading to the motor pump's negative terminal. The positive terminal will have a red dot painted near it. This will be important later. Carefully twist the pair of wires of each motor into a tight braid. Be sure to loosen your grip as you twist to prevent the braid from buckling. Final braids should look as follows. Note the black marks at the end of each negative terminal wire.",
  "31": "Prepare wires:\n8cm, black, solid-solid x1\n5cm, orange, solid-solid x1\n\nNow that we have finished modding and mounting the stepper motors, let’s move on to the motherboard. We’ll begin with routing power, ground, and a switch. Start by trimming the 12V-to-5V converter to 15mm on all leads. Carefully CAREFULLY strip 6mm off the ends, twist, and tin. Solder one side of the black wire to the negative input of the 12V converter. This is the black ground on the 12V side labeled “IN”. Attach a 15mm piece of small heatshrink tubing to cover the joint. Solder the orange wire to the positive 12V input wire of the 12V-to-5V converter. Attach a 15mm piece of small heatshrink to the joint.",
  "32": "Prepare wires:\n28cm, red, solid-solid x1\n28cm, black, solid-solid x1\n23cm, orange, solid-solid x1\n27cm, black, solid-solid x1\n\nCut 200mm of red, orange, and two black silicone wires. We’re going to use these for the 12V and 5V bus headers. Clamp a 20-pin female header to your helping hands. Strip each wire to expose 60mm of copper. Do this by stripping about 6mm from the inner length of 50mm, then gently pull off the silicone wire encasement by hand. Twist and tin the wire. Apply flux to each pin on the 20-pin female header (you can do this with a single drag). Line up the base of one of the tinned wires with one of the edge pins of the 20-pin female header. Apply a bead of solder to anchor the tinned wire to the pin. Carefully drag the soldering iron across all the pins and secure the other edge pin with the wire using a bead of solder.\n\nLoad the soldering iron tip with a bit of solder, then systematically place a bead of solder on each of the 20 pins. This process is a bit difficult to explain (see below), but you’ll want to use a dragging motion perpendicular to the header's length, avoiding direct contact between the soldering iron and the plastic header housing as much as possible. You’ll know a pin has been properly welded to the wire because it will form a small triangular weld that seamlessly joins the pin to the wire. It does not have to be pretty, but each pin MUST be electrically connected to the bus wire. Try your best to emulate the bus header. Repeat the above step for each of the three remaining wires. Really make sure each pin is soldered to the wire. If you mess up, start that part over. It’s very important that these bus headers are functional, as they will supply power to the entire robot. You got this! Once the wires are all headered up, trim the excess wire such that the total length of the wires coming off the header strips is as follows.",
  "33": "Thread a 15mm piece of small heat-shrink onto the wires of the equal-length red and black headers as shown below. Solder the red header to the 5V output positive lead of the 12V-to-5V converter. Solder the black header (the long one) to the converter's 5V output negative. Drag the heat-shrink pieces over the joints and shrink them.",
  "34": "Using helping hands, clamp the orange input positive of the 12V-to-5V converter and the orange header wires together, then solder them. Do the same as above for the black input negative and solder it to the short black header wire. All the headers are now soldered to their respective converter wires, and the full assembly should look like the one below:",
  "35": "Let’s now turn our focus to the motherboard itself. Grab the barrel jack connector and trim the flat ground lead to match the center pin's length. Screw this barrel jack connector into the circular hole at the back of the Module E (motherboard) using needle nose pliers. Once screwed in, rotate the barrel jack such that the ground lead faces toward the closest wall:",
  "36": "Prepare wires:\n5cm, orange, solid-solid x1\n\nObtain the Rocker Switch. Rotate it so that the “O” (OFF) position is on the right. Solder a piece of 50mm orange wire to the rightmost blade on the Rocker Switch, as seen below.",
  "37": "Slot the Rocker Switch into the rectangular hole in the Module E (motherboard) and solder the other end of the 50mm orange wire to the center pin of the barrel jack. Use tweezers to hold the wire securely while soldering, and avoid touching the hot soldering iron to the plastic housing.",
  "38": "Orient the 12V-to-5V Converter as seen in the image below. Slot the 5V headers of the converter into the two slots near the bottom right of the Module E (motherboard).",
  "39": "Slot the 12V headers into their slots near the Rocker Switch and Barrel jack region, placing the orange 12V header closest to the wall. Solder the conjoined orange wires (12v Input from the converter + 12v positive header) to the Rocker Switch blade opposite the already-soldered one. You can trim the solder joint that holds the two\norange wires together so they fit into the hole in the Rocker Switch blade. This can act as a convenient anchor to help solder this tricky part. Use tweezers to assist.",
  "40": "Solder the black conjoined 12V input ground and Ground Header wire to the ground flap on the Barrel Jack. Try your best not to bridge the ground flap to the center positive pin during soldering. The ground flap is electrically connected to the outer casing of the Barrel Jack, so don’t worry if you get solder on this region. All you need to do is avoid making solder bridges between the grounded casing and the center pin. Use tweezers for support and try not to touch the plastic with your hot soldering iron.",
  "41": "Blob a generous amount of black RTV silicone sealant onto the Rocker Switch blades and as much of the barrel jack as you can. Essentially, coat all exposed solder joints with the silicone and allow the material to cure overnight before energizing the device. We have several more parts to assemble and attach to the motherboard before we apply power, so don’t get hasty and let the silicone take its time.",
  "42": "Congrats! You’ve now completed the most difficult part of the entire robot. The remaining soldering will be fairly straightforward, but for now, you can rejoice in a job well done.",
  "43": "Next up, let’s solder the pin headers on each circuit board that will be mounted to the Motherboard. Starting with the Real-Time Clock, grab a strip of right-angle male pin headers and break off enough to fit every hole along the Real-Time Clock board. Solder the pin headers to the Real-Time Clock board as seen below. Secure the right-angle header tightly against the PCB of the Real-Time Clock, apply a small amount of flux to each pin protruding through the PCB, solder the edge pins to anchor the pin header to the PCB, and carefully solder the remaining pins.",
  "44": "Using 95% Ispropanol, brush off any excess flux residue. Wipe off residual alcohol and allow the part a minute to air-dry. Slot a CR1220 battery into the Real-Time Clock's battery holder, then slot the clock as shown in the image below.",
  "45": "Obtain the I2C Multiplexer and break off enough male pin headers to fit the board. Solder the pin headers as shown below, and remove any excess flux with 95% isopropanol.",
  "46": "Next up is the LED Controller Board. This one will require 48 male pin headers in sets of three and two female 6-pin headers.",
  "47": "The first thing we are going to do to this LED Controller Board is remove the built-in current limiting resistor and replace it with our own precision resistor. To do this, apply flux to both sides of the surface-mount resistor and heat both sides with your soldering iron. Once the solder is molten, carefully slide the resistor off its pads as shown below. The resistor may stick to your soldering iron. If this happens, just use your brass tip-cleaning pad to scrape the resistor off the tip.",
  "48": "Obtain the 2.49 Kohms Precision Resistor, bend the leads of the resistor so they are at a right angle to the resistor body, and slot it through the two holes above and below where the surface-mount resistor was.",
  "49": "Flip the board over, bend the resistor leads at a 45-degree angle to the PCB surface, apply a small dot of flux to each resistor hole, and solder the leads to the hole as shown below. Clip the leads once they are soldered.",
  "50": "Break off 16 sets of three male pin headers. Solder them to the board as seen below. Be careful not to flood any of the adjacent holes during soldering, as it is a royal pain to unclog soldered holes. Use ample flux and take your time. Helping hands are handy in this situation. Good luck!",
  "51": "Solder the two 6-pin female headers to the ends of the LED Controller board. Clean off all the excess flux with isopropanol and an old toothbrush. Pat dry and blow out the female headers so no liquid remains in the holes.",
  "52": "Obtain the DRV8871 Motor Controller Board and solder the two screw terminals and 4-pin male header as shown below. Make sure the screw terminals’ ports are facing outward. Clean off excess flux.",
  "53": "Flip the Module E (motherboard) over and obtain the Module F (motherboard rail part). Orient the Module E (motherboard) such that the Rocker Switch and Barrel Jack face toward you. Align the Module F (motherboard rail) at the top of the Module E (motherboard), opposite the switch and barrel, such that the screw holes line up. Drive two 20mm M3 screws on the right side of the Module F (motherboard rail). Screw 10mm M3 female standoffs onto the screws on the opposite side.",
  "54": "Using 20mm M2.5 screws, bolt down the other side of the Module F (motherboard rail) and attach 10mm M2.5 female standoffs as you did in the previous step.",
  "55": "Using 10mm M2.5 Screws, screw the M2.5 female standoffs into the underside of the holes adjacent to those in the step before this. For every screw you run through the Module E (motherboard), attach a corresponding 10mm female standoff to said screw on the opposite side.",
  "56": "Attach five more 10mm M3 screws and their corresponding 10mm M3 female standoffs as shown in the image below. Note the position of these screws, as some holes are meant for M2.5. This awkward screw sizing is an unfortunate choice by the manufacturer of these circuit modules, which we have to consider.",
  "57": "Attach the last four 10mm M2.5 screws to the remaining holes with standoffs on the opposite side.",
  "58": "Mount the I2C Multiplexer to the Module E (motherboard) using 4mm M2.5 screws with the VIN pin nearest to the left wall of the Module E (motherboard) if oriented with the rocker switch facing away from you. Mount the LED Controller Board using 4mm M2.5 screws, and orient it so that the female pin header labeled INPUT faces the rocker switch. The labels for these headers are on the underside of the LED Controller Board.",
  "59": "Mount Module G (DC motor rail) and Module F (motherboard rail). Flip the robot on its side (stepper motor clamp arms facing upward), apply two rubber feet as shown, and route the orange heater and stirrer wires through the left Module E (motherboard) slot (bottom cutout when robot is on its side).",
  "60": "Mount the Stirring Motor Controller to the Motherboard using 4mm M2.5 screws as shown. Attach the positive lead of the stirring fan to the OUT2 screw terminal and the ground wire to the OUT1 screw terminal. Be sure to route the wires between the female standoffs underneath the board so we can keep wires tidy.",
  "61": "Attach the Top Left and Right wires from the Heater Plate to the OUT1 and OUT2 screw terminals on the Heater Controller Board. Attach the Heater Plate Bottom Left/Right wires to the OUT3 and OUT4 screw terminals. The polarity does not matter in this case, as the resistive heating pads will heat regardless of polarity.",
  "62": "Mount the Heater Controller Board to the Module E (motherboard) using 4mm M3 screws as shown below. Route the wires on the inside of the Heater Controller Board’s footprint to keep wires tidy.",
  "63": "Cut a 110mm piece of black-and-orange silicone wire. Strip both sides 6mm and tin one side of each wire. Crimp a male pin connector to the untinned side as shown below.",
  "64": "Connect the male pin side of the two wires from the previous step to the first header holes of the 12V rail and its corresponding ground rail. Match the rail color to the wire color. Snake the wires’ tinned ends underneath the Heater Controller Board, and then attach the black wire to the GND screw terminal and the orange wire to the 12V screw terminal perpendicular to where you mounted the heater plate wires, as shown below. These wires provide the 12V power to the heating pads, so be sure to connect the orange wire to 12V and not the 5V screw terminal.",
  "65": "Mount the Arduino MEGA 2560 Board to the Module E (motherboard) using 4mm M3 screws. One of the corner screws will be a tight fit in between the pin headers, but this will not damage the Arduino.",
  "66": "Obtain and mount it onto the Arduino as shown. Make sure you align the board as seen below. Cut and place a double-sided tape that overlaps the USB-B port of the Arduino. Only remove the bottom protective film of said tape. This is to ensure the underside of the Motor Shield board does not short to the grounded USB port casing.",
  "67": "Cut 120mm of black and orange silicone wire. Strip both sides to 6mm, tin one side, and crimp a male connector onto the other. Attach the tinned side of the orange wire to the Power(+) screw terminal and the tinned side of the black wire to the Power(-) screw terminal of the Motor Shield. This is the main power supply for the DC peristaltic pump motors. Route the two wires under the Heater Controller board, and plug the male connector ends into the 12V orange rail and the Ground Rail as shown below.",
  "68": "Cut 50mm of black and orange silicone wire, strip both ends by 6mm, tin one end, and crimp a male connector onto the other end of each wire. Connect the tinned end of the orange wire to the POWER+ screw terminal of the Stirring Controller Board, and connect the black wire’s tinned end to the POWER- screw terminal. Connect the male pin connector ends of these wires to their respective power rail. The orange wire goes to the 12V rail, and the black wire goes to the Ground Rail, as shown below.",
  "69": "Connect the DC peristaltic pump motors to the Motor Shield. With the robot’s LCD screen facing you, the motors from left to right are M1, M2, M3, and M4. Connect the positive unmarked lead of each motor to its respective screw terminal. These are typically the outermost terminal holes in each pair. The inner terminals are the shared ground, where you connect the marked ground lines of the pump motors. Seen below is a view of the robot from behind. Note the crossed connections to their respective screw terminals. Pay close attention to the polarity of each terminal written on the board as well as the markings on the tinned ends of each pump motor.",
  "70": "Route the four LCD screen wires over the left cutout on the Motherboard. Connect the red wire to the 5V rail and the black wire to the GND Rail. Connect the yellow SDA wire to the SDA0 pin on the I2C Multiplexer Board. Connect the green SCL wire to the SCL0 pin of the multiplexer board.",
  "71": "Route the four button wires from the LCD Faceplate over the left Motherboard cutout. Connect the black wire to the GND Rail. Next, connect the blue wire to digital pin 26 on the Arduino, the white wire to digital pin 24, and the green wire to digital pin 22.",
  "72": "Route the six SD Card Module wires from the LCD Faceplate. Connect the red wire to the 5V rail, the black wire to the GND Rail.\n\nConnect the following to the Arduino MEGA Board as shown below:\n\nbrown wire to digital pin 53\norange wire to digital pin 51\npink wire to digital pin 52\npurple wire to digital pin 50\n\nCS -> Brown\nSCK -> Pink\nMOSI -> Orange\nMISO -> Purple\nVCC -> Red\nGND -> Black (not shown, connects to GND Bus Rail)",
  "73": "Next up are the assorted jumpers that connect data and power to various modules on the motherboard. Starting with the Real-Time Clock module, cut 110mm of red and black silicone wire and then cut 50mm of yellow and green wire. Strip 6mm of each end and attach a female connector to both ends of the green and yellow wires. Attach a female to one end of the red and black wires and a male to the other.",
  "74": "Connect the green wire from the Real-Time Clock’s SCL pin to the I2C Multiplexer’s SCL1 pin. Connect the yellow wire from the Real-Time Clock’s SDA pin to the I2C Multiplexer’s SDA1 pin. Connect the red wire from the Real-Time Clock’s Vin pin to the 5V Bus Rail. Connect the black wire from the Real-TimeClock’s GND pin to the GND Bus Rail.",
  "75": "Next, let’s wire up the I2C Multiplexer. Cut a 120mm piece of yellow and green silicone wire, then cut a 50mm piece of black and red silicone wire. Strip 6mm off each end. Attach a female connector on one end and a male on the other for every piece of wire in this set.",
  "76": "Connect the yellow wire from the I2C Multiplexer SDA pin to the Arduino MEGA’s Digital Pin 20. Connect the green wire from the I2C Multiplexer SCL pin to the Arduino MEGA’s Digital Pin 21. Connect the red wire from the I2C Multiplexer’s VIN pin to the 5V Bus Rail. Connect the black wire from the I2C multiplexer’s GND pin to the GND Bus Rail. Note that these are not numbered SDA/SCL pins; rather, *THE* SDA/SCL pins that connect the multiplexer to the Arduino’s I2C data bus. These pins are located next to the VIN and GND pins on the multiplexer.",
  "77": "Next, we’ll wire up the LED Controller Board. Cut a blue, white, and green silicone wire to a length of 80m. Cut a black and red wire to 60mm. Strip both ends of each wire and attach male connectors to each end.",
  "78": "To wire the LED Controller Boards, we’ll be using the female header strip closest to the I2C Multiplexer. Attach the following wires:\n\nblack wire from LED Controller’s GND to the GND Bus rail.\nred wire from LED Controller’s V+ pin to the 5V Bus rail.\nwhite wire from LED Controller’s DIN pin to Arduino’s Digital 25.\nblue wire from LED Controller’s CLK pin to Arduino’s Digital 27.\ngreen wire from LED Controller’s LAT pin to Arduino Digital 23.",
  "79": "On to the stirring motor controller! Cut 70mm of blue and white silicone wire. Strip 6mm from each end. For each wire, attach a female and a male connector.",
  "80": "Connect the blue wire from the Stirring Motor Controller’s IN1 pin to the Arduino MEGA’s Digital Pin 48. Connect the white wire from the Stirring Motor Controller’s IN2 pin to Arduino Digital Pin 46.",
  "81": "Moving on to the Heater Controller. Some Heater Controller brands have jumpers that need to be removed (see below). If present, first we need to remove the two jumper shorts connecting the ENA and ENB pins on the Heater Controller. There is a small metal bridge you can scoop a sharp pair of tweezers through to pull off the jumper. This allows us to control the power to the heater and turn it off completely when not in use. In the shorted state, the heater controller is constantly energized, while in the un-jumpered state, we’ll route wires from these two pins to the Arduino so we can control when the heater controller needs to be turned on.",
  "82": "Let’s wire the Heater Controller to the Arduino! Cut 140mm of pink, blue, brown, purple, orange, and white wire. Strip the ends of each wire, then attach a female connector to one end and a male connector to the other.",
  "83": "Connect the following:\n\npink wire from Heater Controller ENA to Arduino Digital 44.\nbrown wire from Heater Controller IN1 to Arduino Digital 42.\npurple wire from Heater Controller IN2 to Arduino Digital 40.\norange wire from Heater Controller IN3 to Arduino Digital 38.\nwhite wire from Heater Controller IN4 to Arduino Digital 36.\nblue wire from Heater Controller ENB to Arduino Digital 34.",
  "84": "Cut a 100mm red and a 100mm black wire, strip and twist the ends. To both sides of each wire, add a male pin header.",
  "85": "Connect one end of the black wire to the GND header on the double-row header strip along the Arduino’s side opposite the USB port. Next, connect the other end of the black wire to the GND rail. Connect one end of the red wire to the 5V header on the same double-row header strip as before. These two wires will power the Arduino board directly and are very important.",
  "86": "Next up, let’s wire the Heater Module’s thermometer so the robot knows how hot the heater plate is at any given moment. Cut and strip 260mm of brown silicone wire. Cut and strip 200mm of red and 200mm of black silicone wire. Apply Female headers to one end of each wire and Male headers to the other. Rotate the robot onto its side so that the underside faces you. Locate the three pins on the Heater Module. Connect the brown wire’s female end to the middle of the three pins. Then connect the red and\nblack wires’ female ends to the two pins flanking the brown wire. Depending on how you oriented the thermistor during the Heater Module Assembly step, you may need to flip the red and black wires once the robot can be powered on and tested. Connect the male end of the red and black wires to the 5V Rail and GND Rail, respectively. Connect the brown signal wire’s male end to the Arduino MEGA’s A9 analog pin.",
  "87": "Cut and strip 380mm of pink, red, and black silicone wire. To one end of each wire attach a female header, and to the other attach a male header. Braid the wires loosely to keep them tidy and together. Connect the pink male end to the Arduino MEGA’s A8 analog pin, the red wire to the 5V rail, and the black wire to the GND rail. Connect the pink female end to the middle pin of the thermistor needle assembly, and connect the red and black pins to either adjacent pins on the thermistor needle. We will test the polarities of both the heater plate and the thermistor needle connections later.",
  "88": "The next few steps will focus on the business end of the machine and all the sensors that stare into the culture tube. To begin, obtain the Module L (Module Bay part) and carefully remove any 3D printed support material on or around the bays of the part, as shown below. Use a flathead screwdriver if some of the support material is stuck. Be patient and don’t torque the part’s walls too much.",
  "89": "Obtain the Module M (Blank Sensor Module part). This will be the master module whose magnetic orientation will inform all the other Module Bay Parts. Remove any 3D-printed support material, dab a small amount of black silicone sealant into both magnet holes, and press two 12x6x3mm rectangular magnets into the holes (the two magnets should be on different polarities). Use a chopstick or poking implement to snuggly secure the magnets in the sealant-covered hole. Incubate this part at 37℃ for 1 hour, and then apply some clear tape over the magnet face and wrap that tape around the module once. The tape will act as a divider between the embedded magnets and the loose magnets we will orient.",
  "90": "Select one long face of the Module M (Blank Sensor Module) perpendicular to the magnets and designate that as the TOP. Place a piece of clear tape on that face and label it \"TOP\". This will be the orientation of the magnetic field, which we will use to construct other modules and to embed magnets in the sensor bay itself.",
  "91": "Place two loose 12x6x3mm magnets onto the embedded magnets. Allow them to snap into position naturally.",
  "92": "Place a small bit of silicone sealant into each magnet hole on every face of the Module L (Module Bay part). With the Module M (Blank Sensor Module) oriented so that the word “TOP” faces upward, carefully remove one magnet at a time, being EXTREMELY CAREFUL to maintain its orientation, and embed it into the sensor bay. It’s very important to maintain the orientation so that the Blank Sensor Module is equally attracted to the embedded magnets in each Module L (Module Bay) slot. Incubate the freshly embedded magnets in the Module L (Module Bay part) for 1 hour at 37℃ to cure the silicone sealant. You’ll know you messed up if, after incubating and trying to fit the Module M (Blank Sensor Module) into each slot, one of the sides is repelled instead of attracted. If this happens, I’m sorry, but you’ll have to reprint the Module L (Module Bay part) and try embedding the magnets again, as getting the magnets out of the cured silicone sealant is a nightmare I wish upon no soul. Best of luck, and, as with all the other parts, do take your time.",
  "93": "Mount the completed Module L (Module Bay part) on top of the Module D (Stirrer Base) Assembly as shown below.",
  "94": "Now that we have our shiny new Module Bay, let’s make some modules! Starting with the most important sensor of the whole robot, the Optical Density Module, let’s give the robot the ability to see. Print the Module N (OD Sensor Module part) and glue the 12x6x3mm magnets in using the black silicone sealant so that they attract to the\n\nModule M (Blank Sensor Module) and orient as shown below. The wire cutout should be at the bottom of Module N (OD Sensor Module) when Module M (Blank Sensor Module) pulls it in, provided the magnet orientation is correct.",
  "95": "Obtain the TSL2591 module and flip it around. A small trace needs to be cut to disable the on-board power indicator LED. This is important because we DO NOT want this light to affect the reading of the microbes in the reactor vial. Use a sharp scalpel and carefully cut the tiny linking trace between the pads as shown below.",
  "96": "Solder the pin header strip that comes with this sensor board as shown below. Clean up any flux residue with 95% isopropanol and be extra careful not to smudge or scratch the small sensor window in the center of the board. Variations on the surface of this sensor will greatly impact the uniformity of future measurements.",
  "97": "Cut and strip 33mm of yellow and green silicone wire and 25mm of red and black silicone wire. To the pair of yellow and green wires, add female headers on either end. For the red and black wires, attach a female to one end and a male to the other. Route the wires through the slot of the Module N (OD Sensor Module) and connect the red to Vin, the black to GND, the yellow to SDA, and the green to SCL on the TSL2591 board.",
  "98": "Blob a bit of black silicone sealant onto the inner face of the Module N (OD Sensor Module) and press the TSL2591 firmly onto the blob such that the board is flush with the flat surface of the Module N (OD Sensor Module) body. Flip the Module N (OD Sensor Module) around and blob some sealant along the slot to seal the wires in place, leaving no room for light to enter. Let the silicone cure at room temperature for a few hours, or at 37℃ for at least 1 hour. Before setting the module to cure, ensure the TSL2591 board is firmly pressed down and flat.",
  "99": "While our hands are dirty with silicone sealant, let’s build the 940nm light source for the robot. Print the Module O (LED Module) and remove any supports. Glue in two 12x6x3mm magnets, black silicone sealant, such that they mate with the Module M (Blank Sensor Module) reference. Obtain the 940nm LEDs and cut two 38cm pieces of white silicone wire.",
  "100": "Strip both ends of the white wires. Twist and tin one end of each wire and apply a female header on the other end. Make a mark high up on the longest leg of the 940nm LED to mark the positive leg. Cut down the LED legs to just above the small flattened indentation about 5mm from the body of the LED. Solder the tinned side of each white wire to the legs as shown below.",
  "101": "Press the LED into the back of the Module O (LED Module) using a thin screwdriver. Be careful not to compress the legs or bend them into each other, which will cause a short.",
  "102": "Blob in a bit of black silicone sealant to fill the rest of the Module O’s (LED Module) hole to block light from entering from the back of the LED. Move the wires a little to allow the sealant to envelop both, so there are no gaps where light can enter. Hold the module up to a light source to inspect for haps in the sealant. Allow the sealant to cure at room temperature for a few hours, or for at least 1 hour at 37℃.",
  "103": "Next up, let’s wire the OD sensor and 940nm LED module to the robot! Attach the Module N (OD Sensor Module) to the back bay on the Sensor Bay. Attach the 940nm Module O (LED Module) to the front bay of the Sensor Bay, and put the Module Q (reactor top) on it. Route the wires neatly beneath the robot and connect the wires as follows:\n\nYellow sensor wire to SDA2 on the I2C Multiplexer\nGreen sensor wire to SCL2 on the I2C Multiplexer\nRed sensor wire to 5V rail\nBlack sensor wire to GND Rail\nMarked White LED wire to the Channel Zero positive pin on the LED Driver Board\nUnmarked White LED to Channel Zero negative pin on the LED Driver Board",
  "104": "Next, we will make the LED six-pack on the other side. First, we need to stick the magnets onto Module P (LED SixPack Module) using silicon sealant, following the previous steps, and then place them in a 37℃ incubator for at least an hour.",
  "105": "Take out six LED lights of different colors and simply mark them.",
  "106": "Cut 6 silicon wires of corresponding colors into 32 cm lengths, twist and tin them at one end, and apply a female header at the other end.",
  "107": "Solder the two wires to the two pins according to the corresponding colors, just like step 130, and remember to solder the wire with the longer leg and simply mark it with a pen near the female header. This is the negative pole.",
  "108": "Press all the LEDs into the back of the Module P (LED SixPack Module) using a thin screwdriver. Blob in a bit of black silicone sealant to fill the rest of the LED, then put them in a 37℃ incubator for at least an hour, just like how you did in step 131 and step 132.",
  "109": "After incubating, attach the Module P (LED SixPack Module) to the side bay of the Sensor Bay. Route the wires neatly beneath the robot and connect the wires, the marked wires to Channel One to Six positive pin on LED Driver and board unmarked wires to Channel One to Six negative pin on the LED Driver Board in order.\n\nCongrats on wiring up the robot!",
  "110": "Let’s move to silicone mold casting. In this step, we will cast the robot's tube cap, which requires you to pour a two-part, non-toxic, self-curing silicone base and hardener. Prepare the Module H-K (reactor cap mold, below) and six 14-gauge 4” luer-lock dispensing needles. The needles act as guides for holes through which similarly sized needles can enter the cured cap when the robot is ready to be used. The silicone is fully autoclavable, and this method allows for convenient casting of any shape.\n\nGrab your 3D printed Cap Mold parts (Module H-K) and begin by pushing the needles through the holes in Module H.",
  "111": "Next, assemble the bottom module H with the needles and the two middle molds, modules I and J. Align them in a star pattern where the top is along the line of symmetry; they will only fit one way.",
  "112": "Now it is time to prepare the silicone. If you want to make it black (to block light) you can mix in some black mica powder. In a plastic beaker or cup, add 20 grams of each Base and Hardener. After mixing it all, you can place it in a bag and cut off the corner of the bag for easier dispensing.",
  "113": "Once the silicone fills up the Cap Mold, place the Top (module K) to secure the mold.",
  "114": "Set the mold aside at room temp overnight or incubate at 37℃ for at least two hours. Best results if left overnight so the center parts cure evenly. Once cured, remove the needles from the mold assembly. You can push them out partially, one by one, against a surface, or use pliers to carefully push the needles out. Do not pull by the luer-lock ends, as those are glued on and will rip off. Take your time with this, as the needles are snug. Try not to bend them since you can reuse them. Pinch off the excess around the perimeter of your cap with your fingers. I found this method more effective than a scalpel.",
  "115": "We additionally designed parts to make media bottle tops from silicone and tubing [modules S-V]. These replace commercial bottle tops that tend to leak and are expensive. First, print out your parts and arrange them to prepare to pour the silicon.",
  "116": "Weigh out 20 grams of each of the two silicon parts, pour the silicon until it slightly overflows the container, and place one of the mold tops on.",
  "117": "After the mold cures, assemble the tubes with luer locks to lock them in place. First, place the luer lock at the end of a short tube, then push the tubing through the hole. Trim the tube to be just long enough to pass through the other side without sticking out. Then place the other Luer lock on the other side of the tube and repeat for the second hole. This is fully autoclavable.",
  "118": "We additionally designed modules for light-tight containers for media bottles with holes for the tubing to pass through [Modules W-Y]. The lids snap together after printing and fit snugly on top of the cylinder that holds the media bottle."
 },
 "testing": {
  "1": "Now we need to make sure all the systems function properly. The first thing we need to do is calibrate the pumps so they dispense as close to 1mL as possible per dispensing cycle. To start, let’s cut 60mm segments of silicone tubing and add Barb-To-Male Luer Lock Adapters as shown below. Be sure to push the tubing at least 2mm over the top of the barb to ensure a safe and secure fit. Place the other end of each tube segment onto the nozzles of the DC pump motors.",
  "2": "Using the DOWN button on the EasyEVO interface, scroll to the PRIME PUMPS function and press the blue SELECT button to enter this state. Here, we will test whether all the motors turn as they are supposed to.",
  "3": "Now follow the on-screen instructions, pressing UP or DOWN to jog the motors and pressing select to cycle through each motor. With the robot screen facing you, the pumps are hardcoded left to right as follows:\n\nNEU - neutral media line\nWST - waste removal line\nPOS - positive selection media line\nNEG - negative selection media line\n\nWhen you press DOWN while the current pump is the first pump (NEU, neutral media), it should turn so that the left side of the tubing pulls into the pump and the right side extends outward. This configuration allows liquid to be pumped in from the left side of each pump and out from the right side. Cycle through each pump to ensure the same motion occurs, noting the WST (waste) pump will run twice as long as the others, since we never want to pump in more media than we pump out. If one of the pumps is running backward, simply rotate the stepper motor connector for that pump on the CNC Shield of the EasyEVO. I often find that the first pump, connected to the X-axis on the CNC Shield, is wired in reverse. This is a random error from the manufacturer and will not damage the robot or its motors. Simply flip the connector of any backward-running pump. All of the pumps should look like this when jogged DOWN:",
  "4": "We will now set up a calibration to tune each pump so that a single dispensing cycle delivers 1mL. Start by cutting two 38cm pieces of silicone tubing. Attach a Barb-To-Female Luer Lock connector to one end of each piece of silicone tubing. Connect a Barb-to-Male Luer Lock connector to a 2” 14 Gauge Dispensing Needle. Connect this barbed needle assembly to one of the silicone tubing’s female connectors. Connect the left side of the first pump to the tubing without the needle and the right side of the connector to the tubing with the needle. Place the tubing end without the needle into a beaker of water as shown below:",
  "5": "With the EasyEVO in the PRIME PUMPS state and the current pump set to NEU, press the DOWN button to begin sucking up water from the beaker. Keep pressing until water is ejected from the needle. Keep the needle over the beaker to avoid making a mess. This primes the needle intake line with liquid so that subsequent dispensations will be only ejections from the needle, not dead volume. Once primed, hold the needle in a 15mL conical tube and dispense exactly 10 times into the tube. Read the liquid level after dispensing. It should be near 10mL.",
  "6": "If the liquid level is exactly at the 10mL line, you can reverse the liquid back into the beaker to clear both tubing lines of water, then reconnect to the next pump and repeat this process by pressing the SELECT button to select the next pump in the series. If the liquid level is not close enough to the 10mL mark, please open the EasyEVO code in the Arduino IDE and locate the pump calibration variables as shown below.",
  "7": "Adjust the motor step value for each wayward pump, upload the modified code to the EasyEVO, return to PRIME PUMPS, and repeat the 10x dispensation for that pump until you are satisfied with the calibration. I would recommend going up and down in steps of 20 or 40 if you are within 0.5mL of the 10mL mark, and go up or down by 100 units if you are off by more than 1mL. Be sure to use the same value for the Forward and Reverse step variables for each pump so that removal and dispensation are at the same pace. The Reverse variable has a negative sign, so do not forget to check whether it is there. Lastly, please use a twisted paper towel to remove any water in the tube in between measurements so you do not accidentally bias the set of dispensations with residual water. This process is tedious but critical for the robot, and you only need to do this once (unless you alter the electronics). It’s always good to check the calibration every now and then.",
  "8": "Next, let’s get out of the PRIME PUMPS state and back into the MENU state by holding down the SELECT button. Scroll over to HEATER TEST. Don’t enter the state yet, as we need to set up the test environment. Obtain a 50mL centrifuge tube or graduated cylinder, a 90mL glass EasyEVO vial, a cast silicone rubber cap, a magnetic stir bar, a beaker of ice water, and the thermometer needle we made a while back, already wired to the robot.",
  "9": "Fill the 50mL centrifuge tube or graduated cylinder to the 50mL mark with water and dispense it into the glass via. Place the silicone cap on the vial and wipe the vial's walls to remove any fingerprints. If the vial is dirty, wash it with dish soap until spotless, then place the filled vial into the EasyEVO reactor well. Place the thermometer needle into the beaker of ice water, stir around for a few seconds to chill the thermometer body, and then press SELECT on the HEATER TEST menu item to enter this test state. You will immediately hear a quiet whirring sound as the heater test begins. This sound is coming from the magnetic stirrer as it spins. We will now focus on the values on the screen. We want to see the Media Temp value drop to near zero (0 °C to 2 °C), and the HTR (heater temp) value rise slowly along with the PID value, indicating the heater is warming up, and the thermometer needle senses the cold of the ice water. If the Media Temp value is increasing instead of decreasing, you wired the thermometer needle backward. To alleviate this, reverse the RED and BLACK header wires connected to the needle. If the HTR value is going down instead of up, you wired the heater thermometer backward. To alleviate this, exit the HEATER TEST state by holding down the SELECT button, remove the thermometer needle from the ice water, remove the glass vial from the reactor well, turn the robot on its side, and swap the RED and BLACK wires on the Heater Module beneath the robot (part where the fan is housed). Set the test environment back to its previous state and check whether the heater temp is rising now.",
  "10": "While in the HEATER TEST state, let’s check if the stirring is working. Carefully lift the robot's head to expose a section of the glass vial in the reactor well. Look for a vortex cone as a sign that the stirrer is working.",
  "11": "Next, we need to check the optics. To do this, we first shut off the robot, remove the microSD card from the LCD Faceplate (the card is removed by a push to release the card; do not rip the card out directly), and format the card so we have a freshly formatted card for our data recording to store into. I strongly recommend the 8GB Industrial SD cards, as they are designed for such applications and will not burn in memory sectors, which can lead to data loss after many cycles of writing and reading. Place the formatted card back into the EasyEVO and turn on the robot. If the card was formatted successfully and seated properly in the LCD faceplate, the MENU state should appear on the screen. If there is an error or the card is not seated properly, the machine will show SD CARD ERROR on the screen. Scroll down to the INCUBATE state and press SELECT.",
  "12": "The INCUBATE state does what it says: incubates a batch culture with no inputs or outputs, maintaining a stable temperature and optical density. To get the most out of our optical density sensor, we need to ensure it is not completely saturated by the 940nm LED light. To adjust this zero calibration point, let’s plug the robot into our laptops via the USB cable and open up the EasyEVOTypeS.ino project file once more. In the file, locate the OD940LEDBrightness variable and adjust that number, higher = brighter, until the IR value reads just a hair below 37889 and the OD value is showing changes at the 0.0001 mark. This will allow the sensor's full dynamic range, from optically clear to complete darkness. The TSL2591 has a tremendous dynamic range and can sense very subtle variations in light. Take your time uploading and verifying this zero value, as it is critical to the proper functioning of the EasyEVO.",
  "13": "You have now fully assembled and calibrated your EasyEVO. CONGRATULATIONS!!! The next step is to run an INCUBATION run to obtain a reference curve for your cell line. Let’s start by setting up the glass vial for incubation. To do this, obtain ten Barb-To-Male Luer Lock connectors, five 6cm pieces of silicone tubing, five 2” 14 Gauge Needles, a 90mL glass reactor vial, a cast silicone stopper, and a magnetic stir bar. Place a luer connector on each end of every piece of silicone tubing. Connect each piece of tubing to a needle. Place the magnetic stir bar in the glass vial. Cap the glass vial with the silicone cap. Slide the needles into the holes along the edge of the silicone cap, then connect the Luer Lock caps at each end of the silicone tubing protruding from the silicone cap, as shown below. Leave the center hole open; this is where the thermometer needle will slide in after surface sterilization in bleach later.",
  "14": "Wrap the tube assembly in aluminum foil, apply a piece of autoclave tape to the foil, and autoclave at 121 °C for at least 20 minutes.",
  "15": "While the vial assembly autoclaves, prepare a 10% dilution of household bleach in a 15mL conical tube to the 15mL mark. Spray the thermometer needle with 70% isopropanol, wipe down the needle, then dip the needle into the solution to the collar and let it sterilize at room temperature under sterile conditions for at least 20 minutes. After 20 minutes have elapsed, move the needle into a sterile 15mL tube with sterile distilled water to rinse off the bleach. Let the needle sit in the distilled water until the glass vial assembly is autoclaved.",
  "16": "Open the assembly under sterile conditions and fill the vial with exactly 50mL of microbial media. Resuspend a single colony of freshly grown bacteria from a solid plate of the same media you wish to use in your EasyEVO growth curve experiment into 500 μL of liquid media. Vortex briefly and dispense 50 μL of this resuspended culture into your reactor vial. Place the cap back onto the reactor vial, ensuring a snug and flush fit.",
  "17": "Remove one of the luer caps from the reactor vial assembly and replace it with a 0.22 μm syringe filter. This will act as a filtered air vent during culture. Slide the thermometer needle into the center of the silicone cap on the vial assembly. You are now ready to begin culturing your microbes in the EasyEVO.",
  "18": "Turn off the EasyEVO, remove the microSD card from the LCD Faceplate, format the card, and reinsert it into the LCD Faceplate. Place the reactor vial into the reactor well of the EasyEVO. Connect the needle thermometer wires and turn on the EasyEVO. Select the INCUBATE state and let the robot do its magic.",
  "19": "Allow the robot to bake the cells overnight, recording the culture parameters every second. If you use NEB Turbo E. coli cells, you may start to see changes in the OD within two hours of inoculation from a cold start. We’ll graph the data to check all the systems tomorrow morning.",
  "20": "The following morning, exit out of the INCUBATE state by holding down the SELECT button, turn off the EasyEVO, remove the microSD card from the LCD faceplate, and graph the resulting data. Use the UPTIME column as the time start and the OD940 column for the optical density. Produce a scatter plot of the data and adjust the X-axis to have major gridlines every 3600 seconds (1 hour), and the Y-axis to be scaled from 0 to 2.000. See if you spot any severe jitter, line noise, or large random spikes in the data, as this may indicate a faulty connection on the motherboard. If all went well, you should have a classic growth curve like the image below:",
  "21": "Next, plot the heater plate temperature, media temperature, and ambient temperature over time. Make separate graphs for each and look for anything out of place. Here is what a properly functioning robot temp chart looks like:"
 }
};
